cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "algebraic-plus-three-ramsey: algebraic points on a circle plus at most three arbitrary points are Euclidean Ramsey (Lean 4)"
version: "1.0.0"
authors:
  - family-names: "Strinz"
    given-names: "Will"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/wstrinz/algebraic-plus-three-ramsey"
abstract: >-
  A Lean 4 proof that a finite set of points on a circle in the plane (any centre
  and radius), all but at most three of which have real algebraic coordinates, is
  Euclidean Ramsey (Theorem B(b) of the note in spherical-ramsey v1.1.0,
  doi:10.5281/zenodo.23396387, of which this is the Lean proof ported to the Lean
  module system and Lean v4.35.0-rc2, with the same statements and proofs). With
  the Möbius invariance proved in mobius-ramsey (Theorem C; the combination is by
  hand), it settles the class that Theorem A.10 of the appendix of Pálvölgyi's
  arXiv:2609.23327 leaves undecided: such sets are Ramsey. The proof builds an explicit
  certificate for the field criterion of OpenAI's classification of finite
  Euclidean Ramsey configurations (openai/math, result 172) and concludes with
  OpenAI's Lean proof of its sufficiency, which it trusts; the case of at most six
  points is the six-concyclic-points theorem (prior work of JD-Jones-ASES).
  Verification. Every theorem depends only on the axioms propext,
  Classical.choice and Quot.sound; the toolchain's lake comparator (Lean
  v4.35.0-rc2) accepts the proofs against a Mathlib-only statement file with the
  Lean, NanoDa and con-ron kernels, and refuses five negative controls. AI
  disclosure. The author, Will Strinz, is the only human contributor. The proofs,
  the Lean port, the checks and the documentation were produced by AI agents
  (Claude Opus 5.5 and Claude Sonnet 5.5, Anthropic, in Claude Code) under his
  direction; no human expert has reviewed this work. See DISCLOSURE.md.
  Licence. A mixed tree: this project's own files are under the MIT License; the
  OpenAI-derived Lean files and two PalomarTemplate scripts are under the Apache
  License 2.0. NOTICE lists which file is under which licence.
keywords:
  - Euclidean Ramsey theory
  - cyclic configurations
  - algebraic numbers
  - Lean 4
  - formal verification
  - Mathlib
